❌ “Just checking session is valid” is NOT enough
✅ You need JWT (or sessionBinding) because you must cryptographically bind the request to the session

Let’s break it clearly so you can defend it confidently 👇

🧠 1. What “session check” actually does

If you only do:

isSessionValid(sessionId)

👉 You are only proving:

“User is logged in”

❌ What it does NOT prove
❌ This request belongs to that session
❌ This payload was created in that session
❌ This request was not replayed or injected
❌ This request was not modified in transit

👉 It’s just authentication, not binding

🔥 2. The Core Problem

Your system is doing client-side signing + encryption

👉 That means:

Server cannot trust anything blindly
Everything must be cryptographically verifiable
🚨 3. Attack Without JWT / Binding
Scenario: Replay / Injection attack
User logs in → valid session
Attacker captures a valid signed request
Attacker replays it with:
same sessionId OR another valid session

Server checks:

session is valid ✅
signature is valid ✅

👉 Boom — request accepted ❌

⚠️ Why this happens

Because nothing ties:

Session ↔ Request ↔ Payload
✅ 4. What JWT Binding Fixes

Your JWT:

{
  "sid": "sessionId",
  "rid": "requestId",
  "nonce": "xyz",
  "exp": 1710000000
}

Now server verifies:

sid == sessionId
rid == requestId
nonce == stored nonce

👉 This ensures:

“This request was issued for THIS session, THIS requestId, THIS nonce”

🔗 5. Real Difference (This is your key answer)
❌ Without JWT (only session check)
User is valid
BUT request is untrusted
✅ With JWT binding
User is valid
AND request is cryptographically tied to that user/session
🧩 6. Why DB alone is not enough

You might think:

“I already store requestId + sessionId in DB”

👉 But DB is:

server-side state
NOT part of signature
❌ Problem

Attacker can:

reuse signed payload
attach it to another requestId

👉 DB check alone cannot detect this reliably

✅ JWT is inside signature
Sign(... + bindingToken + ...)

👉 Now:

If anything changes → signature breaks
🔐 7. Deep Insight (Architect-level answer)

Tell this:

“Session validation is a runtime check.
JWT binding is a cryptographic guarantee.”

🧠 8. Why Client Must Verify JWT

Another key point:

👉 If you don’t use JWT:

Client doesn’t know if request context is legit
Malicious JS can inject fake request
With JWT:

Client verifies:

verify(bindingToken, serverPublicKey)

👉 Ensures:

“This request context came from server”

🔄 9. Full Comparison
Feature	Session Check Only	JWT Binding
User authenticated	✅	✅
Request integrity	❌	✅
Replay protection	❌	✅
Request-session binding	❌	✅
Tamper detection	❌	✅
Client trust in context	❌	✅
🎯 10. One-Liner Answer (Use This)

If architect asks:

👉 “Why not just session validation?”

Say:

“Session validation only proves the user is logged in.
It does not bind the request to that session.
We use a signed JWT and include it in the signature to cryptographically tie the payload, request, and session together, preventing replay and injection attacks.”

🔥 11. Simple Analogy

Without JWT:

“User is logged in”
But anyone can submit requests on their behalf

With JWT:

“This exact request was issued for this user and signed accordingly”
